WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:08.100
Choose a random street, in a random neighborhood,&nbsp;
on a random day in Toronto, and odds are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:13.500
the first person you see – not including&nbsp;
tourists! – won’t have been born in Canada.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:20.580
51% – or 1.4 of its 2.7 million residents – are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:26.760
immigrants – significantly more than Los&nbsp;
Angeles, San Francisco, or even New York.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:36.600
Last year, Canada welcomed 437,000 new permanent&nbsp;
residents – 1% of its entire national population.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:44.640
When you count all new residents – permanent&nbsp;
and not – the nation grew by 2.7% – making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:51.300
it the 16th fastest-growing country in the world&nbsp;
and the only one of these not in Africa or Asia.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.500
And yet, Canada is just getting started.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:03.840
13 years from now, immigrants are expected to make&nbsp;
up 30% of the population, compared to 23% today.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.540
Now compare that to the “melting pot”&nbsp;
known as the United States of America.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.620
When you ask Americans how many immigrants they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.860
think are in the country,&nbsp;
the average answer is 40%.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:24.660
In fact, every demographic group – White,&nbsp;
Black, Latino, Asian, Liberal, Conservative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:30.540
even immigrants themselves – vastly&nbsp;
overestimates the true number… just 14%.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.160
Here’s a list of the 34 countries and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.000
territories with a larger share of&nbsp;
immigrants per capita than the U.S.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.820
After removing small countries –&nbsp;
those with a population under 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.920
million – you can see what actually&nbsp;
sets America apart: its size.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.680
The United States is the largest country that&nbsp;
accepts a significant share of immigrants.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.260
But, as a share of the population,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:03.420
Canada, far more than America,&nbsp;
is truly a nation of immigrants.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.980
Even more striking is the large&nbsp;
number of young engineers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.240
doctors, and scientists settling&nbsp;
in Canada over the United States.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.700
Nearly the same number of documented&nbsp;
employment-based immigrants entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.400
Canada as the U.S. in 2022, despite&nbsp;
being only one-tenth as large.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.820
No offense to our exceptionally&nbsp;
friendly Northern neighbors, but… why?

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:35.400
The simple answer is that immigrating to&nbsp;
the United States is an insanely difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:42.060
stressful, costly, lengthy, and arbitrary&nbsp;
process, while immigrating to Canada… isn’t.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.220
To see how, let’s compare the journeys&nbsp;
of two hypothetical immigrants.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:54.960
“Sanjay” and “Rohan” were both born in India.&nbsp;
They both scored in the 95th-percentile on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.160
their college entrance exams, and both&nbsp;
received scholarships to study abroad.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:04.620
Sanjay, to Columbia in New York;&nbsp;
and Rohan to McGill in Montreal.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:12.420
After graduating, Rohan is allowed to work&nbsp;
in Canada for three additional years. Sanjay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.940
one year, or three with a STEM major.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:22.260
So far, so good. But then the paths of our&nbsp;
two immigrants rapidly begin to diverge.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.100
Assuming Sanjay doesn’t have a million dollars&nbsp;
to invest or a close U.S. Citizen relative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.700
he’ll be thrown into the grueling H-1B gauntlet.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.720
The H-1B is a high-skill visa for in-demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.020
“specialty” occupations like&nbsp;
programmers and accountants.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.660
To try for one, Sanjay will need to find&nbsp;
an employer who agrees to pay him no less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.620
than the “prevailing wage” for that&nbsp;
job and to sponsor his application.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:54.120
But that alone is no guarantee – far from it.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:59.580
For the last twenty years, Congress&nbsp;
has limited the number of H-1Bs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.500
65,000 per year, with an additional&nbsp;
20,000 for those with advanced degrees.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:11.160
And in every one of those years – typically&nbsp;
in less than 5 days – more than the maximum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.440
85,000 people have applied.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.940
Sanjay, therefore, will have to play the lottery.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.820
Despite attending one of America’s&nbsp;
best schools, receiving a perfect GPA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.960
mastering English, and acclimating&nbsp;
well, his name will be thrown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.380
along with thousands of others,&nbsp;
into a random number generator.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.480
At this point, he’ll likely lose&nbsp;
and be kicked back to square one.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:43.380
In a good year, 43% of applicants are&nbsp;
selected. In a bad one, like last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:49.680
just 14.6% – though the Department of Homeland&nbsp;
Security attributes this to a rise in fraud.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.260
After educating Sanjay for years,&nbsp;
America will unintentionally gift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.080
his valuable knowledge and&nbsp;
labor to another country.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.120
At the exact moment he’s ready to&nbsp;
contribute to the U.S. economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.040
he’ll be forced to either leave or go&nbsp;
back to school and delay his employment.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.400
But let’s assume he’s one of the lucky few.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.240
Having been selected, U.S. Citizenship and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.380
Immigration Services will look – for&nbsp;
the first time – at his application.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.700
If he’s then approved, Sanjay will join the tiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.520
elite circle of America’s&nbsp;
most fortunate immigrants.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:36.540
In 2021, the median wage for all U.S. workers&nbsp;
was $45,000. For H-1B holders, it was $108,000.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:43.980
Even many of the lowest-paid H-1B holders&nbsp;
make more than 75% of all Americans.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.720
If he chooses to, Sanjay will&nbsp;
live a life of abundance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.640
He’ll drive a nice car, live in a big&nbsp;
house, and eat at nice restaurants.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.020
But although he’ll likely be&nbsp;
financially comfortable, legally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.560
he’ll exist in a perpetual&nbsp;
state of anxious precarity.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:08.400
That’s because the H-1B is not a green&nbsp;
card. It’s a temporary work visa.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:14.040
For one, this means travel is&nbsp;
more complex. Leaving the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.800
even for a few days, requires permission.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:22.800
And, though unlikely, Sanjay will only be&nbsp;
one irritable border guard away from being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.340
locked out of the place he’s spent&nbsp;
most of a decade building a life in.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:34.200
Secondly, Sanjay doesn’t have permission to work.&nbsp;
He has permission to work for a specific employer.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:39.900
If he’s fired or laid off, he’ll have&nbsp;
60 harrowing days to find a new company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:45.360
willing to sponsor his visa, or else pack up&nbsp;
and, along with his family, leave the country.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:50.880
Now, to escape this absurdity,&nbsp;
Sanjay needs a green card.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.080
And, in theory, he’s already eligible to apply.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.920
But only his employer can apply on his behalf.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.240
In other words, the freedom&nbsp;
to leave one’s employer must,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.480
in most cases, be approved… by that very employer.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:11.880
This process is not cheap. And&nbsp;
only his company is allowed to pay.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:17.280
So, unless Sanjay’s skills are&nbsp;
truly irreplaceable or his employer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.980
unusually generous, he may have to wait.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:24.840
After 3 more years, he’ll need to renew his H-1B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.700
which can only be done once and&nbsp;
since 2004, requires – for no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:34.200
clear reason – traveling back to your home&nbsp;
country’s U.S. consulate and re-entering.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.120
If Sanjay hasn’t found an employer&nbsp;
willing to sponsor his green card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:42.060
after another 3 years, he’ll be forced to leave.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.540
But, again, let’s assume he’s lucky.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.600
After finding a sponsor, proving he won’t&nbsp;
negatively impact the wages of U.S. workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:57.060
and that there are no other qualified&nbsp;
candidates, Sanjay will finally… start waiting.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.440
Because, not only does Congress&nbsp;
limit the number of green cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:07.020
it also sets a limit per country. And that&nbsp;
number is the same regardless of its population.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:13.080
1.4 billion people live in India. Five million in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:16.440
Norway. But the two countries&nbsp;
have the same annual limit.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:24.420
Naturally, more Indians and Chinese apply than&nbsp;
Norwegians, which creates a backlog of the former.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:32.340
As of July 2023, these are the “priority dates”&nbsp;
for this type of green card. What this means is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:38.820
that Indians who applied on the 1st of January,&nbsp;
2009 are just now receiving their green cards.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:42.840
Across all nationalities and all categories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:47.160
these are the average wait&nbsp;
times to receive one as of 2018.

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:55.260
22% waited between 10 and 19&nbsp;
years. 5% waited 20 years or more!

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:00.960
In the meantime, Sanjay will be&nbsp;
stuck in limbo – a temporary worker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.620
tied to a single employer,&nbsp;
potentially for decades.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:10.560
During this time, he may watch as someone&nbsp;
from a smaller country enters the U.S. for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.160
the first time, receives a green&nbsp;
card, and then even citizenship.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.460
Now, back to Rohan.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:23.280
Because he’s worked in Canada, Rohan is&nbsp;
eligible to apply for “Express Entry”.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.800
It – unlike the H-1B lottery –&nbsp;
is transparent and predictable.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.080
It’s based on a point system.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:36.300
Anyone can go online right now, enter&nbsp;
things like your English proficiency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:41.820
work experience, and age, and immediately&nbsp;
see your score. It takes about two minutes.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:47.100
Every few weeks, the government invites&nbsp;
everyone with a certain score to apply.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:51.000
Previous cut-offs are listed&nbsp;
clearly on the website.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:56.940
If Rohan has a job offer in Canada,&nbsp;
he’ll very likely be approved.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.560
After that, not only will he not&nbsp;
be tied to a specific employer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.660
but he won’t receive a work visa at&nbsp;
all. He’ll receive permanent residency.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:12.480
And because Canada has no discriminatory&nbsp;
per-country limit, he’ll receive it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:17.580
immediately and be treated the same as&nbsp;
anyone from Norway, Nigeria, or Nepal.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:23.040
In other words, Rohan and his family&nbsp;
will live with a degree of security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:29.520
and stability that Sanjay may not achieve for&nbsp;
another decade or longer. And that’s assuming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:35.160
Sanjay won the lottery and only has to wait&nbsp;
a very optimistic 4 years for a green card.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:42.240
When viewed this way, it’s really not surprising&nbsp;
that so many high-skill workers immigrate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:49.560
Canada. What’s surprising is that, despite this&nbsp;
Kafkaesque bureaucracy, many still prefer America.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.760
On this, the data is pretty unambiguous.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:11:01.320
In a 2021 Gallup survey, of the 900 million&nbsp;
people worldwide who wish to immigrate, nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:07.260
20% said the United States was their first choice&nbsp;
destination – far more than any other country.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.600
And among high-skill immigrants,&nbsp;
that number is likely much higher.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.780
The number one reason is money.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:22.080
Most H-1B jobs are business, science,&nbsp;
engineering, or computer-related.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:29.580
And, to be frank, Canadian tech salaries are&nbsp;
laughable by comparison. We’re talking less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:34.020
than half the money for the same experience,&nbsp;
with the same responsibilities, and title.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:40.140
An L5 Software Developer at Canada’s&nbsp;
Shopify makes something in the area of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:50.400
$121,000 US Dollars. An L4 at Google&nbsp;
makes $264,000 and an L5 $367,000.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:57.420
The higher you go on the org chart, the larger&nbsp;
the gap between U.S. and Canadian wages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:02.100
The most well-compensated software&nbsp;
engineers at Facebook, Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:10.320
and Apple make $1-2 million a year. Those at&nbsp;
Shopify make something like 15-30% as much.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:15.180
For this reason alone, a shocking&nbsp;
number of graduates from Canada’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:18.900
top universities pack up and move&nbsp;
South at the first opportunity.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.620
Then you have Canadian housing.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:29.280
An entire video should be devoted to this topic,&nbsp;
but for now, this graph should do the trick.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:37.020
This is the growth in U.S. incomes since 2005&nbsp;
and this is the growth in U.S. housing prices.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:43.560
Alright now here are Canadian&nbsp;
incomes, and Canadian housing.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:50.100
No one has ever called San Francisco an&nbsp;
affordable place to live. But Toronto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.660
and Vancouver aren’t much better, yet&nbsp;
their salaries are dramatically lower.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:02.460
In 2021, nearly half of new immigrants to&nbsp;
Canada settled in Ontario. And of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:05.700
the vast majority, in one city: Toronto.

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:10.380
That’s a problem because the average&nbsp;
selling price of a home across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:17.340
whole province of Ontario is $928,000.&nbsp;
In British Columbia, over a million.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:23.160
In other words, housing prices provide&nbsp;
immigrants with plenty of reasons not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:28.680
settle in Canada and high salaries, plenty&nbsp;
of reasons to settle in the United States.

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:30.600
…They just can’t.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:38.940
America’s current immigration policies were mostly&nbsp;
set in 1990 – when just 15% of households had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:45.180
personal computer. This was a time when virtually&nbsp;
none of the current H-1B jobs even existed.

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:51.240
Whatever flavor of reform you prefer,&nbsp;
we’re clearly past due for an update.

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:57.120
And until that day comes, Canada is very&nbsp;
happily filling this gap in the market.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:03.720
Just this year, for instance, it announced a&nbsp;
plan to allow H-1B workers to move to Canada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:07.080
where they’d be immediately&nbsp;
unshackled from their employer.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:13.620
This strategy of leveraging its proximity to&nbsp;
and similarities with the United States only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:18.060
works because the American immigration&nbsp;
system is so unbelievably broken.

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:23.460
Canada need not address its severe&nbsp;
housing crisis or do the difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:26.220
work of nurturing a Silicon Valley of the North.

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:32.580
Whenever a Harvard graduate loses the H-1B&nbsp;
lottery or an Indian national is stuck waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:38.700
a decade or longer for a green card, Canada&nbsp;
need only do what America won’t: let them in.

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:42.360
Now, some may argue that American immigration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:46.080
isn’t broken so much as it has&nbsp;
chosen different priorities.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:51.000
In 2021, 60% of new permanent residents in America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:55.080
were sponsored by family&nbsp;
and 26% by their employer.

00:14:56.040 --> 00:15:00.300
In Canada, the two categories&nbsp;
are almost perfectly reversed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:05.340
There, 20% were family-based&nbsp;
and 62% for employment.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:09.900
It may appear that America’s&nbsp;
goal is to unite families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:14.220
while Canada aims – first and&nbsp;
foremost – to strengthen its economy.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:19.500
But what these numbers hide is the scale&nbsp;
of each country’s immigrant population.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:25.140
True, a smaller share of new Canadian&nbsp;
immigrants are joining their families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:29.400
but only because Canada welcomes&nbsp;
so many immigrants in total.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:34.860
In fact, per capita, it has more family-sponsored&nbsp;
permanent residents than the U.S.

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:38.520
The same is true of humanitarian immigrants.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:43.260
Canada admits nearly twice&nbsp;
as many refugees per capita.

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:49.200
It isn’t pro-wealthy immigrants to the&nbsp;
exclusion of others. By most measures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:51.300
it’s pro-immigrant, full-stop.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:57.960
Today, for example, nearly 70% ‘disagree’&nbsp;
that “Immigration levels are too high”.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:00.960
Perhaps even more surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:05.220
a majority in all three of its largest&nbsp;
political parties support immigration.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:10.920
These surveys suggest the country has&nbsp;
cultivated support for a multicultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:16.560
society as much as a mere willingness to&nbsp;
tolerate newcomers when economically convenient.

